hrough practical scenarios and interviews.","concept_explanation":"Principle: Object-oriented design assigns cohesive responsibilities to objects while protecting invariants through encapsulation and clear collaboration. Example: Model a course enrolment domain where a learner cannot enrol twice and capacity cannot become negative. Workplace method: Identify domain responsibilities, model state and behaviour, prefer composition, define contracts and test invariants. Example: Use Classes, records, interfaces and collections and retain evidence of the result. Quality and interview check: Verify the outcome, explain trade-offs and avoid: Creating inheritance hierarchies for reuse alone or exposing mutable fields publicly. Example: Compare abstraction, encapsulation, inheritance and polymorphism using one domain example.","key_terms":[{"term":"Java","definition":"A practical capability used in object-oriented design work.","example":"Model a course enrolment domain where a learner cannot enrol twice and capacity cannot become negative."},{"term":"Object-Oriented Programming","definition":"A practical capability used in object-oriented design work.","example":"Model a course enrolment domain where a learner cannot enrol twice and capacity cannot become negative."}]},"principles":{"rules":[{"rule_number":1,"title":"Principle","explanation":"Object-oriented design assigns cohesive responsibilities to objects while protecting invariants through encapsulation and clear collaboration.","why_it_works":"Example: Model a course enrolment domain where a learner cannot enrol twice and capacity cannot become negative.","exception":null},{"rule_number":2,"title":"Workplace method","explanation":"Identify domain responsibilities, model state and behaviour, prefer composition, define contracts and test invariants.","why_it_works":"Example: Use Classes, records, interfaces and collections and retain evidence of the result.","exception":null},{"rule_number":3,"title":"Quality and interview check","explanation":"Verify the outcome, explain trade-offs and avoid: Creating inheritance hierarchies for reuse alone or exposing mutable fields publicly.","why_it_works":"Example: Compare abstraction, encapsulation, inheritance and polymorphism using one domain example.","exception":null}],"important_distinctions":[]},"application_method":{"method_title":"Learn, configure, validate","method_overview":"Connect the idea to a practical workflow, then validate the outcome.","steps":[{"step_number":1,"title":"Understand","instruction":"Identify the process goal, actors, inputs and expected outcome.","reason":"A clear process prevents incorrect work.","student_check":"Can you explain the goal in one sentence?"},{"step_number":2,"title":"Apply","instruction":"Complete this workplace lab: Model a course enrolment domain where a learner cannot enrol twice and capacity cannot become negative.","reason":"Hands-on execution turns knowledge into a reusable skill.","student_check":"Did you complete every required part?"},{"step_number":3,"title":"Validate","instruction":"Test the result, review the evidence and correct any gap.","reason":"Validation makes the work reliable and interview-ready.","student_check":"Does the result satisfy the stated outcome?"}],"use_when":"Use this workflow for assess work.","do_not_use_when":"Do not expose confidential information or skip verification.","faster_method":"Avoid this common mistake: Creating inheritance hierarchies for reuse alone or exposing mutable fields publicly."},"worked_examples":[{"example_number":1,"title":"Guided practice","difficulty":"Foundation","question_or_scenario":"Complete this workplace lab: Model a course enrolment domain where a learner cannot enrol twice and capacity cannot become negative.","given_information":["Java","Object-Oriented Programming"],"reasoning_steps":[{"step_number":1,"explanation":"Review the assess requirements and define the expected result."},{"step_number":2,"explanation":"Model a cou