e method: Define resource and status semantics, validate request DTOs, implement service rules, persist safely and return consistent errors. Example: Use Spring Boot, Spring Web and Bean Validation and retain evidence of the result. Quality and interview check: Verify the outcome, explain trade-offs and avoid: Putting all logic in controllers, returning 200 for every outcome or exposing persistence entities directly. Example: Trace an HTTP request through controller, service and repository layers.","key_terms":[{"term":"Spring Boot","definition":"A practical capability used in spring boot rest apis work.","example":"Create enrolment endpoints that return 201 on creation, 404 for missing resources and 409 for a duplicate enrolment."},{"term":"REST APIs","definition":"A practical capability used in spring boot rest apis work.","example":"Create enrolment endpoints that return 201 on creation, 404 for missing resources and 409 for a duplicate enrolment."},{"term":"Java","definition":"A practical capability used in spring boot rest apis work.","example":"Create enrolment endpoints that return 201 on creation, 404 for missing resources and 409 for a duplicate enrolment."}]},"principles":{"rules":[{"rule_number":1,"title":"Principle","explanation":"A REST API exposes resource-oriented operations through HTTP contracts while separating transport, application and persistence responsibilities.","why_it_works":"Example: Create enrolment endpoints that return 201 on creation, 404 for missing resources and 409 for a duplicate enrolment.","exception":null},{"rule_number":2,"title":"Workplace method","explanation":"Define resource and status semantics, validate request DTOs, implement service rules, persist safely and return consistent errors.","why_it_works":"Example: Use Spring Boot, Spring Web and Bean Validation and retain evidence of the result.","exception":null},{"rule_number":3,"title":"Quality and interview check","explanation":"Verify the outcome, explain trade-offs and avoid: Putting all logic in controllers, returning 200 for every outcome or exposing persistence entities directly.","why_it_works":"Example: Trace an HTTP request through controller, service and repository layers.","exception":null}],"important_distinctions":[]},"application_method":{"method_title":"Learn, configure, validate","method_overview":"Connect the idea to a practical workflow, then validate the outcome.","steps":[{"step_number":1,"title":"Understand","instruction":"Identify the process goal, actors, inputs and expected outcome.","reason":"A clear process prevents incorrect work.","student_check":"Can you explain the goal in one sentence?"},{"step_number":2,"title":"Apply","instruction":"Practise this workflow: Define resource and status semantics, validate request DTOs, implement service rules, persist safely and return consistent errors.","reason":"Hands-on execution turns knowledge into a reusable skill.","student_check":"Did you complete every required part?"},{"step_number":3,"title":"Validate","instruction":"Test the result, review the evidence and correct any gap.","reason":"Validation makes the work reliable and interview-ready.","student_check":"Does the result satisfy the stated outcome?"}],"use_when":"Use this workflow for understand work.","do_not_use_when":"Do not expose confidential information or skip verification.","faster_method":"Avoid this common mistake: Putting all logic in controllers, returning 200 for every outcome or exposing persistence entities directly."},"worked_examples":[{"example_number":1,"title":"Guided practice","difficulty":"Foundation to applied","question_or_scenario":"Practise this workflow: Define resource and status semantics, validate request DTOs, implement service rules, persist safely and return consistent errors.","given_information":["Spring Boot","REST APIs","Java"],"reasoning_steps":[{"step_number":1,"explanation":"Review the understand requirements and define the expected result."},{"step_number":2,"explanation":"Create enrolment endpoints that return 201 on creation, 404 for missing resources and 409 for a duplicate enrolment."},{"step_number":3,"explanation":"Validate the outcome. Avoid this common mistake: Putting all logic in controllers, returning 200 for every outcome or exposing persistence entities directly."}],"final_answer":"Define resource and status semantics, validate request DTOs, implement service rules, persist safely and return consistent errors.","why_the_answer_is_correct":"The result follows the defined process, covers the required elements and includes a validation step.","learning_takeaway":"Avoid this common mistake: Putting all logic in controllers, returning 200 for every outcome or exposing persistence entities directly."}],"usage_comparisons":[],"common_mistakes":[{"mistake_number":1,"title":"Acting before understanding","incorrect_approach":"Starting immediately without confirming scope, inputs and success criteria.","why_students_make_it":"Speed can feel more important than process discipline.","why_it_is_wrong":"An incomplete process creates unreliable results and avoidable rework.","correction":"Define the outcome, complete the workflow, and validate it once.","prevention_tip":"Avoid this common mistake: Putting all logic in controllers, returning 200 for every outcome or exposing persistence entities directly."}],"memory_support":{"memory_aid":"Avoid this common mistake: Putting all logic in controllers, returning 200 for every outcome or exposing persistence entities directly.","mental_model":"Concept → Practice → Proof","quick_recall_note":"Understand → Apply → Validate"},"placement_support":{"question_identification_signals":["Understand","Spring Boot REST APIs: Core Concepts","Spring Boot","REST APIs"],"time_management_tip":"Avoid this common mistake: Putting all logic in controllers, returning 200 for every outcome or exposing persistence entities directly.","exam_traps":["Skipping context","Ignoring edge cases or validation"],"in